WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.376 --> 00:00:08.838
I was supposed to go
in the very first session.

00:00:10.382 --> 00:00:12.008
And I had a plan.

00:00:12.384 --> 00:00:16.388
And that plan was I'd do TED,
I'd see Vancouver,

00:00:16.429 --> 00:00:19.015
I'd chill out, I'd get this thing done,

00:00:19.015 --> 00:00:20.559
then I’d relax.

00:00:20.600 --> 00:00:25.313
I'd listen to other people's speeches,
rob their best ideas,

00:00:25.313 --> 00:00:29.276
bring them home and sound
incredibly, incredibly brainy.

00:00:30.277 --> 00:00:32.529
And then your man, Chris,
rings me last weekend.

00:00:32.529 --> 00:00:35.240
He says, "You know
that last thing, the first thing?

00:00:35.282 --> 00:00:36.866
Why don't you go last?"

00:00:36.908 --> 00:00:38.827
I'm like, "Oh, man."

00:00:38.868 --> 00:00:45.208
That brought to mind the words
of the great American philosopher,

00:00:45.208 --> 00:00:46.459
Mike Tyson.

00:00:46.459 --> 00:00:48.670
(Laughter)

00:00:48.670 --> 00:00:54.634
Who said of plans, "Everybody has a plan
until they get a punch in the face."

00:00:54.634 --> 00:00:56.303
(Laughter)

00:00:56.344 --> 00:01:01.057
And of course, you know,
doing the last gig at TED,

00:01:01.057 --> 00:01:03.518
it's not about the pressure,
that's not the problem.

00:01:03.560 --> 00:01:05.061
The pressure isn't the problem.

00:01:05.103 --> 00:01:06.813
The problem is the sobriety.

00:01:06.813 --> 00:01:09.899
(Laughter)

00:01:09.899 --> 00:01:12.319
But the speeches have been amazing, right?

00:01:12.319 --> 00:01:14.404
And if I could kind of
encapsulate them all,

00:01:14.446 --> 00:01:16.615
it's really been about,
as far as I can see,

00:01:16.615 --> 00:01:21.828
the pace of change and what that's doing
to us demographically, politically,

00:01:21.828 --> 00:01:23.580
socially, economically.

00:01:23.622 --> 00:01:25.206
Extraordinary stuff.

00:01:25.624 --> 00:01:29.377
And I'd now like to quote from

00:01:29.419 --> 00:01:32.756
a great liberal, democrat,

00:01:32.756 --> 00:01:35.425
a friend of TED,

00:01:35.425 --> 00:01:36.801
Vladimir Lenin.

00:01:37.385 --> 00:01:38.928
(Laughter)

00:01:40.513 --> 00:01:43.808
Who said of change and crises

00:01:43.808 --> 00:01:47.896
that there are decades
when nothing happens

00:01:47.896 --> 00:01:51.941
and there are weeks when decades happen.

00:01:51.941 --> 00:01:55.528
And we’re living through those weeks,
you get that feeling,

00:01:55.528 --> 00:01:58.990
and it’s really difficult to know
where to start to think about these ...

00:01:58.990 --> 00:02:01.284
How do you analyze this stuff?

00:02:01.326 --> 00:02:06.456
Now, if I was an American economist

00:02:06.456 --> 00:02:08.333
or a Canadian economist

00:02:08.375 --> 00:02:12.337
or, God forbid, an English economist --

00:02:12.337 --> 00:02:15.423
Actually the nice thing is
they're not good at economics anymore.

00:02:15.840 --> 00:02:17.217
It's so beautiful, isn't it?

00:02:17.258 --> 00:02:19.135
(Laughter)

00:02:19.135 --> 00:02:21.221
Praise the Lord. Praise the Lord.

00:02:21.262 --> 00:02:22.263
(Laughter)

00:02:22.263 --> 00:02:25.100
But I'd come here armed
with the tools of my trade.

00:02:25.100 --> 00:02:29.270
You know, the graphs and the charts
and the maths and all that good stuff.

00:02:30.271 --> 00:02:31.648
But I'm an Irish economist,

00:02:31.690 --> 00:02:34.776
so I'm only going to come here armed

00:02:34.818 --> 00:02:38.905
with some lines and some verses of poetry.

00:02:39.364 --> 00:02:40.532
(Laughter)

00:02:40.532 --> 00:02:41.533
Yeah.

00:02:41.533 --> 00:02:42.617
(Applause)

00:02:42.617 --> 00:02:44.744
Thank you very much,
the poets in the corner.

00:02:44.744 --> 00:02:46.037
We're a small minority.

00:02:46.037 --> 00:02:47.163
(Laughter)

00:02:47.163 --> 00:02:48.623
But perfectly formed.

00:02:48.665 --> 00:02:53.336
Now, I want to talk to you about a poem
that was written in 1919

00:02:53.378 --> 00:02:58.508
called "The Second Coming"
by W.B. Yeats, our national poet.

00:02:59.217 --> 00:03:01.094
And the fascinating thing about crisis,

00:03:01.094 --> 00:03:02.971
so in 1919, 100-odd years ago --

00:03:03.012 --> 00:03:06.307
the fascinating thing about crisis
is that every generation

00:03:06.349 --> 00:03:08.768
feels that their crisis is the big one.

00:03:08.768 --> 00:03:10.895
We're kind of narcissistic
about it, right?

00:03:10.937 --> 00:03:12.856
But every generation experiences crisis.

00:03:12.856 --> 00:03:15.984
Every generation, our parents,
our grandparents, they all did.

00:03:15.984 --> 00:03:18.987
How we deal with the crisis

00:03:18.987 --> 00:03:22.741
is the definitive issue
for our generation.

00:03:23.324 --> 00:03:27.912
So Yates is sitting in Dublin, 1919.

00:03:27.912 --> 00:03:30.081
He's trying to make sense
of the world, right?

00:03:30.081 --> 00:03:31.750
His world is changing rapidly.

00:03:31.791 --> 00:03:33.710
The German Empire is over.

00:03:34.377 --> 00:03:36.463
The Austrian Empire is over.

00:03:36.463 --> 00:03:38.506
The Ottoman Empire is over.

00:03:39.048 --> 00:03:43.511
Ireland has declared a war
of independence against Britain,

00:03:43.511 --> 00:03:45.889
which, if you were a betting man,

00:03:45.930 --> 00:03:47.515
you wouldn't really give us,

00:03:47.557 --> 00:03:49.976
not our side, you wouldn't
have given us good odds.

00:03:49.976 --> 00:03:53.229
And Yeats is trying to figure out
not just what is happening,

00:03:53.271 --> 00:03:55.356
but what is likely to happen.

00:03:55.899 --> 00:03:58.568
And he writes these words,
and just listen to them

00:03:58.568 --> 00:04:00.737
and imagine they were written now.

00:04:01.237 --> 00:04:02.947
“The Second Coming.”

00:04:02.947 --> 00:04:06.159
“Turning and turning 
in the widening gyre /

00:04:06.201 --> 00:04:09.245
The falcon cannot hear the falconer; /

00:04:09.245 --> 00:04:14.459
Things fall apart; 
the centre cannot hold; /

00:04:14.459 --> 00:04:17.921
Mere anarchy is loosed on the world, /

00:04:17.962 --> 00:04:20.799
A blood-dimmed tide is loosed,

00:04:20.840 --> 00:04:22.884
and everywhere /

00:04:22.926 --> 00:04:27.055
The procession of innocence is drowned; /

00:04:27.055 --> 00:04:30.558
The best lack all conviction,

00:04:30.934 --> 00:04:32.268
the worst /

00:04:32.268 --> 00:04:36.898
Are full of passionate intensity.”

00:04:36.898 --> 00:04:38.691
Just let those words lie.

00:04:40.610 --> 00:04:42.904
“Things fall apart,

00:04:42.904 --> 00:04:46.991
the best people lack conviction,

00:04:46.991 --> 00:04:50.954
the worst are full
of passionate intensity.”

00:04:50.954 --> 00:04:54.541
Now, this is Yeats writing 100 years ago,

00:04:54.541 --> 00:04:58.711
and the historical rhythm and repetition
is clear, I think, to all of us.

00:04:58.753 --> 00:05:02.048
But what really interests me
as an economist

00:05:02.090 --> 00:05:04.843
is the contrast between
what Yeats, the poet,

00:05:04.884 --> 00:05:07.011
was saying back then

00:05:07.011 --> 00:05:08.388
and what all the economists,

00:05:08.388 --> 00:05:12.225
the people who were employed to think
about the future, were saying.

00:05:12.267 --> 00:05:14.811
So, Yeats said,

00:05:14.811 --> 00:05:16.563
"The center will not hold."

00:05:16.604 --> 00:05:18.857
Three years after he wrote this,

00:05:18.857 --> 00:05:21.150
three years, three to four years,

00:05:21.192 --> 00:05:24.237
Mussolini was in power in Italy,

00:05:24.237 --> 00:05:28.074
Stalin was on the ascendancy
in the Soviet Union

00:05:28.116 --> 00:05:33.037
and a little, small, unprepossessing man
with a mustache called Adolf Hitler

00:05:33.037 --> 00:05:36.541
had just orchestrated a putsch in Munich.

00:05:37.876 --> 00:05:39.878
Yates was right.

00:05:40.879 --> 00:05:42.130
Yates was right.

00:05:42.171 --> 00:05:44.549
And what were all the economists
saying back then?

00:05:45.466 --> 00:05:47.886
The people who were paid
to think about the future?

00:05:47.927 --> 00:05:51.180
They were all saying, "Oh, don't worry,
we'll go back to the gold --"

00:05:51.222 --> 00:05:52.640
Not all, the vast majority --

00:05:52.640 --> 00:05:54.559
"We will go back to the gold standard.

00:05:55.101 --> 00:05:56.728
We'll trade again.

00:05:56.769 --> 00:05:58.855
Germany will pay all its reparations

00:05:58.897 --> 00:06:03.735
and the First World War will have been
the war to end all wars."

00:06:04.819 --> 00:06:06.321
How wrong could they be?

00:06:07.280 --> 00:06:09.115
So what's always bugged me

00:06:09.157 --> 00:06:14.746
is why did the poet get things
so right at a tipping point,

00:06:14.787 --> 00:06:18.291
and the economists get things so wrong?

00:06:18.833 --> 00:06:23.463
And I believe it is because the poet,
the artist, the musician,

00:06:23.504 --> 00:06:27.550
these types of people give themselves,
at a tipping point,

00:06:27.550 --> 00:06:31.930
the permission to think unconventionally.

00:06:32.972 --> 00:06:35.016
They see the world differently.

00:06:35.391 --> 00:06:38.895
Do we value the unconventional thinker?

00:06:38.895 --> 00:06:40.104
Do we?

00:06:40.146 --> 00:06:43.191
And maybe the best way
to answer that question

00:06:43.232 --> 00:06:46.361
is to go all the way back to school,

00:06:46.361 --> 00:06:48.821
to the place we begin to learn.

00:06:49.238 --> 00:06:54.410
Now just bring yourselves back
to your school days.

00:06:54.911 --> 00:06:56.287
Remember yourself at 13.

00:06:56.329 --> 00:06:58.164
Just remember the person you were.

00:06:58.748 --> 00:07:00.166
I remember the classroom.

00:07:00.166 --> 00:07:02.627
I remember the teachers,
I remember the building,

00:07:02.669 --> 00:07:04.837
I remember the break,
I remember everything.

00:07:04.879 --> 00:07:07.423
I remember the sports day,
I remember the whole thing.

00:07:07.423 --> 00:07:11.052
In fact, there's a gang of five of us
who've hung around

00:07:11.052 --> 00:07:12.261
since we were in school.

00:07:12.303 --> 00:07:14.347
And those five lads I was in school with

00:07:14.347 --> 00:07:18.142
spent the entire of our school years
looking out the window

00:07:18.685 --> 00:07:20.228
They just didn't get it.

00:07:20.269 --> 00:07:21.980
They just didn't get school at all.

00:07:21.980 --> 00:07:24.565
And they've gone on to have
incredibly successful lives

00:07:24.607 --> 00:07:26.484
in their own individual ways.

00:07:27.276 --> 00:07:28.778
But their intelligence,

00:07:28.778 --> 00:07:30.989
their form of intelligence,
was not recognized.

00:07:31.030 --> 00:07:32.323
And as we get older,

00:07:32.323 --> 00:07:36.327
we realize the world is full
of various different intelligences.

00:07:36.619 --> 00:07:38.371
But back in school,

00:07:38.371 --> 00:07:43.835
we only really recognized
one type of intelligence.

00:07:43.835 --> 00:07:48.798
And that was like the little kid who could
come in -- big, wide-eyed kid, right? --

00:07:48.840 --> 00:07:53.177
he’d come in, could absorb all this
information into his head or her head,

00:07:53.177 --> 00:07:55.013
opened some sort of weird compartment,

00:07:55.054 --> 00:07:57.432
stuff it all in, stuff,
stuff, stuff it all in.

00:07:57.473 --> 00:07:59.851
And then, in early June,

00:07:59.851 --> 00:08:01.811
when the weather gets good in Ireland --

00:08:01.811 --> 00:08:05.106
and I presume it’s the same
all over the place except for Vancouver --

00:08:05.106 --> 00:08:06.482
write like bejesus, right?

00:08:07.233 --> 00:08:10.028
That was the type
of intelligence we rewarded.

00:08:10.069 --> 00:08:13.740
It's like getting a prize for being
a walking filing cabinet, right?

00:08:13.781 --> 00:08:17.118
(Laughter)

00:08:17.660 --> 00:08:19.704
That's one intelligence.

00:08:19.704 --> 00:08:22.623
It is an intelligence,
but it's only one type of intelligence.

00:08:22.623 --> 00:08:24.834
And what about all
the other kids over here,

00:08:24.834 --> 00:08:26.377
the kids who didn’t get it? ...

00:08:26.586 --> 00:08:28.921
They weren't not only recognized,

00:08:28.921 --> 00:08:32.133
their intelligence was besmirched,

00:08:32.133 --> 00:08:34.343
humiliated

00:08:34.385 --> 00:08:35.511
and belittled.

00:08:36.846 --> 00:08:38.347
My daughter is dyslexic.

00:08:38.347 --> 00:08:41.642
She hated every single day of school.

00:08:42.018 --> 00:08:43.519
And as a result of this system,

00:08:43.561 --> 00:08:45.855
we have a strange thing in our society

00:08:45.897 --> 00:08:50.943
where there are thousands
of incredibly clever people

00:08:50.985 --> 00:08:53.946
who left school feeling stupid.

00:08:54.322 --> 00:08:56.991
But the corollary is also the case.

00:08:57.033 --> 00:09:01.120
There are many
actually quite stupid people --

00:09:01.788 --> 00:09:04.707
(Laughter)

00:09:04.749 --> 00:09:06.667
You know where I'm going --

00:09:06.709 --> 00:09:09.670
Who left school feeling very, very clever.

00:09:09.670 --> 00:09:11.923
And those kids, when they
were really good kids,

00:09:11.964 --> 00:09:14.008
they used to get the best marks in school.

00:09:14.050 --> 00:09:16.344
In Ireland, we have
a little star over the right.

00:09:16.385 --> 00:09:18.930
And of course, the teacher
told them they were clever,

00:09:18.930 --> 00:09:20.181
and the priests, the nuns,

00:09:20.181 --> 00:09:23.643
and of course, their mummy
told them they were clever, right?

00:09:23.643 --> 00:09:25.770
If you want to get a sense
of an Irish mummy,

00:09:25.770 --> 00:09:27.522
an Irish mummy is the sort of mother

00:09:27.563 --> 00:09:30.525
that makes an American
soccer mom look unambitious.

00:09:30.566 --> 00:09:34.320
(Laughter)

00:09:34.320 --> 00:09:36.864
I know, I have one, right?

00:09:37.198 --> 00:09:40.535
I am the son of a retired schoolteacher.

00:09:40.535 --> 00:09:44.163
If anybody else in the room is the son
or daughter of a schoolteacher,

00:09:44.205 --> 00:09:48.584
we can form a self-help group later on
to deal with our trauma, right?

00:09:49.168 --> 00:09:51.045
But those sort of kids,

00:09:51.087 --> 00:09:53.297
they do extremely,
extremely well in school,

00:09:53.297 --> 00:09:55.216
then they do very, very well in college.

00:09:55.258 --> 00:09:57.593
And then they get on,
because they do very, well,

00:09:57.635 --> 00:10:00.847
on to the graduate trainee programs
and they join the big banks,

00:10:00.847 --> 00:10:04.350
and they join the big firms
and they join insurance companies,

00:10:04.392 --> 00:10:07.687
they join the consultancies,
and they're on the fast track.

00:10:07.728 --> 00:10:09.564
They’re on the fast track career-wise.

00:10:09.605 --> 00:10:12.233
And something weird happens
as their career progresses.

00:10:13.234 --> 00:10:16.362
We like to think
that we surround ourselves

00:10:16.404 --> 00:10:18.447
with people who think differently,

00:10:18.489 --> 00:10:19.615
but we don't.

00:10:19.657 --> 00:10:22.034
There's something
called confirmation bias.

00:10:22.076 --> 00:10:27.373
We actually surround ourselves
with people who confirm our biases.

00:10:27.373 --> 00:10:31.586
And how this works
in institutions is the following.

00:10:32.295 --> 00:10:36.299
We end up employing people
who think like us, right?

00:10:36.632 --> 00:10:38.801
And like, if you imagine
an interview process,

00:10:38.801 --> 00:10:42.388
so the person goes into the interview
and there's two clever persons saying,

00:10:42.388 --> 00:10:45.099
"No, no, you're clever."
"You're clever, you're amazing."

00:10:45.099 --> 00:10:47.685
"You've got that grade."
"You went to that university."

00:10:47.727 --> 00:10:51.022
"I read that paper you wrote."
"You're clever, clever, amazing."

00:10:51.022 --> 00:10:52.982
"Feck it, just have the job."

00:10:54.442 --> 00:10:56.068
So the interview becomes --

00:10:56.110 --> 00:10:58.112
By the way, I said “feck” there.

00:10:59.530 --> 00:11:00.823
Not the other word.

00:11:01.741 --> 00:11:04.869
Feck is a sort of a linguistic
version of a white lie.

00:11:05.244 --> 00:11:06.454
OK?

00:11:06.454 --> 00:11:07.788
It's used at home.

00:11:07.830 --> 00:11:09.707
Now, what actually happens is

00:11:09.707 --> 00:11:12.043
the interview process
just becomes like a Tinder

00:11:12.043 --> 00:11:14.170
for people who can do algebra.

00:11:14.212 --> 00:11:15.379
OK.

00:11:15.379 --> 00:11:16.547
(Laughter)

00:11:16.547 --> 00:11:18.257
So they all go into the institution

00:11:18.299 --> 00:11:20.593
and then what you get, you get groupthink

00:11:20.593 --> 00:11:22.762
because everyone's thinking the same way.

00:11:22.762 --> 00:11:23.930
And of course,

00:11:23.971 --> 00:11:27.558
because these people have always been
the clever kids with the right answer,

00:11:27.558 --> 00:11:30.603
they defined themselves,
"I'm the person with the right answer."

00:11:30.603 --> 00:11:34.023
And what happens when you’re the person
who always has the right answer?

00:11:34.023 --> 00:11:35.691
It’s very hard to be wrong.

00:11:36.567 --> 00:11:39.278
So it breeds a sort of an overconfidence.

00:11:39.278 --> 00:11:41.364
And we know that overconfidence

00:11:41.364 --> 00:11:43.699
and overconfident people

00:11:43.741 --> 00:11:48.162
can really overestimate
their competence's critical moments.

00:11:48.204 --> 00:11:52.124
The thing comes from a thing
called Dunning-Kruger in psychology.

00:11:52.124 --> 00:11:53.292
It's a great story.

00:11:53.292 --> 00:11:56.170
A fella goes in to rob a bank in America.

00:11:56.212 --> 00:11:58.881
Somewhere, I think Pittsburgh,
down there somewhere.

00:11:59.548 --> 00:12:02.635
In the '90s, a lad goes
into rob a bank, OK?

00:12:02.677 --> 00:12:06.180
He runs into the bank ...
he’s got no mask, no balaclava,

00:12:06.222 --> 00:12:07.390
no nothing, right?

00:12:07.390 --> 00:12:09.600
The security camera looks at him.

00:12:10.101 --> 00:12:11.602
And he winks at it.

00:12:11.978 --> 00:12:14.814
He winks again, big smile,
"Hi, how are you?"

00:12:14.814 --> 00:12:17.108
He goes in, holds up the bank,
robs the thing.

00:12:17.650 --> 00:12:18.818
Goes off.

00:12:18.818 --> 00:12:20.236
Coppers, come in,

00:12:20.278 --> 00:12:21.946
look at the securities.

00:12:22.863 --> 00:12:25.032
"See this geezer,
just waved to the camera.

00:12:25.074 --> 00:12:26.242
Does anyone know him?"

00:12:26.242 --> 00:12:29.245
"Oh, yeah, he lives around the corner,
around the second block,

00:12:29.245 --> 00:12:30.663
sixth house, up third floor."

00:12:30.663 --> 00:12:32.999
So they arrive and bang on the door,

00:12:33.040 --> 00:12:36.210
the man's there, I presume,
kind of eating a takeaway or something.

00:12:36.210 --> 00:12:39.714
And copper said, "We want
to bang you off for the bank robbery."

00:12:39.714 --> 00:12:42.008
And he said, "How do you mean,
the bank robbery?"

00:12:42.008 --> 00:12:44.927
“We’ve got you on camera
for the bank robbery ... here we go.”

00:12:44.927 --> 00:12:47.388
"How do you mean?"
"We've got you!"

00:12:47.430 --> 00:12:51.309
“How could you possibly know?
I wore the juice!”

00:12:53.394 --> 00:12:55.146
Copper looks at him, says, “What?”

00:12:55.646 --> 00:12:57.440
Do you remember when we were kids?

00:12:57.440 --> 00:12:58.858
Invisible ink?

00:13:00.526 --> 00:13:02.987
And you put lemon juice
on the invisible ink --

00:13:03.029 --> 00:13:04.655
this is a true story --

00:13:04.697 --> 00:13:05.865
and you disappear.

00:13:05.865 --> 00:13:09.076
Your man covered his face with lemon juice

00:13:09.118 --> 00:13:10.995
and he thought he was invisible.

00:13:12.246 --> 00:13:15.916
The overconfident
overestimating their competence.

00:13:15.958 --> 00:13:18.169
Now, when the psychologists
thought about this,

00:13:18.169 --> 00:13:20.129
they actually did lots and lots of tests.

00:13:20.171 --> 00:13:22.923
And apparently it's true,
that this is a thing in society.

00:13:22.965 --> 00:13:24.133
And surprise, surprise,

00:13:24.133 --> 00:13:28.554
the Dunning-Kruger effect
is much more prevalent

00:13:28.596 --> 00:13:30.222
in men than women.

00:13:30.973 --> 00:13:32.391
Who would have known?

00:13:32.433 --> 00:13:33.434
(Laughter)

00:13:33.434 --> 00:13:34.602
Shock horror.

00:13:34.643 --> 00:13:36.771
I can see it in my own family.

00:13:36.771 --> 00:13:40.775
Our son, for example, comes home,
teenager, after doing an exam.

00:13:41.150 --> 00:13:43.152
Hasn't done a tap of work, right?

00:13:43.194 --> 00:13:46.447
Comes in the door and everyone
in the family's in the kitchen saying,

00:13:46.447 --> 00:13:48.032
"How did you do in your exam?"

00:13:48.074 --> 00:13:51.994
And he says, "Oh, Jesus,
I aced that, man, no problem."

00:13:52.036 --> 00:13:53.329
And he fails all the time.

00:13:53.371 --> 00:13:54.955
(Laughter)

00:13:54.997 --> 00:13:56.207
All the time.

00:13:56.207 --> 00:13:58.751
So you see these things
and that again it happens.

00:13:58.793 --> 00:13:59.919
And of course,

00:13:59.919 --> 00:14:03.214
how this happens in institutions
is you get very overconfident people,

00:14:03.255 --> 00:14:05.800
very intelligent, can't make mistakes.

00:14:06.175 --> 00:14:10.971
And we saw that
in the 2008 financial crisis.

00:14:11.263 --> 00:14:14.767
2008, the biggest financial crisis
the world has ever seen.

00:14:14.767 --> 00:14:19.188
And the vast majority of economists
and the Fed and the Bank of England

00:14:19.230 --> 00:14:22.149
and the European Central Bank
and Wall Street

00:14:22.191 --> 00:14:23.943
missed the whole thing.

00:14:24.276 --> 00:14:27.780
In fact, the Queen of England
went to the LSC

00:14:27.822 --> 00:14:31.283
about two or three months
after the crash and she said,

00:14:31.325 --> 00:14:34.245
"If you chaps were so clever,

00:14:34.286 --> 00:14:36.997
how come none of you saw this coming?"

00:14:36.997 --> 00:14:38.416
(Laughter)

00:14:38.457 --> 00:14:39.917
And she was right.

00:14:39.917 --> 00:14:41.335
(Laughter)

00:14:41.836 --> 00:14:44.255
Because they all were wearing the juice.

00:14:44.296 --> 00:14:46.340
(Laughter)

00:14:46.382 --> 00:14:48.050
The economics juice.

00:14:49.802 --> 00:14:54.432
Now, JK Galbraith,
very famous Canadian economist.

00:14:54.432 --> 00:14:59.145
But Galbraith said something fascinating
about the conventional person.

00:14:59.645 --> 00:15:04.442
He said, "When faced with the choice
between changing his mind

00:15:04.442 --> 00:15:07.695
and finding the proof not to do so,

00:15:07.736 --> 00:15:13.868
the conventional man always gets busy
looking for the proof."

00:15:14.660 --> 00:15:20.458
And that's when we make big mistakes
at these critical moments.

00:15:21.083 --> 00:15:23.752
Leonard Cohen,

00:15:23.794 --> 00:15:25.671
the Canadian poet,

00:15:25.713 --> 00:15:27.798
put it differently, the same idea.

00:15:28.257 --> 00:15:29.800
Cohen said,

00:15:29.800 --> 00:15:33.179
"There is a crack in everything.

00:15:33.637 --> 00:15:37.433
And that is how the light gets in."

00:15:39.185 --> 00:15:42.563
What Cohen was saying to us was,

00:15:42.563 --> 00:15:45.983
look for the cracks, look into the cracks.

00:15:46.025 --> 00:15:48.986
That's where we'll see the big picture.

00:15:50.196 --> 00:15:52.573
So let’s just leave the Canadian poet,

00:15:52.615 --> 00:15:54.283
go back to an Irish poet.

00:15:55.451 --> 00:15:57.661
The words of "The Second Coming" again,

00:15:57.703 --> 00:15:59.497
our world right now.

00:16:00.372 --> 00:16:03.459
“Turning and turning 
in the widening gyre /

00:16:03.459 --> 00:16:06.837
The falcon cannot hear the falconer; /

00:16:06.837 --> 00:16:10.633
Things fall apart; 
the center cannot hold; /

00:16:10.674 --> 00:16:13.469
Mere anarchy is loosed on the world, /

00:16:13.511 --> 00:16:16.138
A blood-dimmed tide is loosed,

00:16:16.180 --> 00:16:21.018
and everywhere, / The procession
of innocence is drowned.”

00:16:21.018 --> 00:16:23.103
And why, says Yeats?

00:16:23.103 --> 00:16:26.774
Because the best lack all conviction

00:16:26.774 --> 00:16:31.237
and the worst are full
of passionate intensity.

00:16:32.613 --> 00:16:34.490
Now apply that to our world.

00:16:35.074 --> 00:16:36.951
And if you, at this tipping point say,

00:16:36.992 --> 00:16:39.411
"Nah, you know, it's not my problem,

00:16:39.453 --> 00:16:41.664
I lack all conviction."

00:16:41.664 --> 00:16:42.831
Right?

00:16:42.831 --> 00:16:44.083
“I go and watch baseball,”

00:16:44.083 --> 00:16:46.877
or rounders or whatever that game is
you guys watch, right?

00:16:46.877 --> 00:16:49.672
But if you lack all conviction,

00:16:49.713 --> 00:16:54.176
what actually happens
is the worst people in our society,

00:16:54.218 --> 00:16:58.138
with their passionate intensity,
their certitude, their simple ideas,

00:16:58.180 --> 00:17:01.308
they will win the day
at this tipping point.

00:17:01.684 --> 00:17:05.229
And they will win the day
because the best people back away

00:17:05.229 --> 00:17:07.147
from the responsibility.

00:17:07.648 --> 00:17:10.150
And what Yates was also saying
about the best people

00:17:10.192 --> 00:17:11.360
was the following.

00:17:11.402 --> 00:17:13.696
He said, mandate the best.

00:17:15.197 --> 00:17:19.326
The poets, the artists,

00:17:19.368 --> 00:17:21.287
the musicians,

00:17:21.328 --> 00:17:26.208
because they are the people
who see the possibilities.

00:17:26.250 --> 00:17:28.460
They see the possibilities

00:17:28.502 --> 00:17:31.380
because they see the world
from a different angle.

00:17:31.380 --> 00:17:34.883
They have to be part of the solution.

00:17:36.677 --> 00:17:39.513
So my idea

00:17:39.513 --> 00:17:44.560
that I think is worth spreading
is the following.

00:17:45.436 --> 00:17:52.109
If you want to understand the world
a little bit more clearly,

00:17:52.151 --> 00:17:57.906
listen less to my tribe, the economists,

00:17:57.948 --> 00:18:02.828
and listen more
to Yeats's tribe, the poets.

00:18:03.454 --> 00:18:04.663
Thank you very much.

00:18:04.705 --> 00:18:07.333
(Applause)

00:18:07.333 --> 00:18:08.959
Thank you very much.

